{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790960930,"nonce":"2vo2Zh6rkJ9WmJGBWks/WrJ1cDk=","title":"Exit from inside a host call deadlocks: TLS destructors run TieredRuntime::shutdown while call_raw holds its lock","message":"When a call made through TieredRuntime::call_raw ends in libc exit (for example a Python uncaught exception's zb_exit(1)), thread-local destructors run TieredRuntime::shutdown on the same thread while call_raw still holds the runtime lock, and the process deadlocks instead of exiting. Seen by caribou, which now leaves a runtime that is mid-call to the process exit. Shutdown from a destructor must not take a lock its own thread holds (skip shutdown when a call is in progress on this thread, or make the lock reentrant for shutdown). Related: 30bd7ba (exit while an LLVM promotion is in flight).","files":null}]}